A||||5||Bill Osmer||Speedo Sensor Help!!!||||11||
Z||000000||Bill Osmer||10-28-2000||10:03 PM||||Help - I just completed changing my manual 4-spd transmission's oil. While removing the speedo sensor, the gear that's on the sensor shaft stayed in the transmission - and "floated away" - Now I'm wondering what will happen if I don't remove it. And even worse, now I don't have the use of my speedometer - where can I get another speedo sensor gear??<P>------------------<BR>Bill Osmer<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumcardsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||11
Z||000001||Rodrv6||10-29-2000||12:05 AM||||Even though the gear is plastic, I don't think it would be a good idea to leave it in there.  It is going to get chewed up by the other gears inside, and while it MIGHT not hurt anything, it is a risk.  I had the same thing happen to mine and after I drained the fluid I was able to fish out the gear with some wire bent into a hook at the end.  I'm just paranoid enough that if I hadn't been able to get it out I was going to remove the transmission and split the case to get it.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||10-29-2000||12:55 PM||||Oh boy! A friend of mine dropped the gear while checking the oil in his 86 2M4. He came to me a week later and said his speedo didn't work since checking the fluid. I asked him if he checked the gear, and he said "what gear?". Well a few weeks later he could no longer shift into R or 5. I pulled the selector cover off the top of the trans and there was a large chunk of plastic jammed in the linkage. We removed that and all is fine for now. I think we will also remove the side cover and fish out any debris from there. If at all possible, you should try to get it out. Good Luck! You can get a new gear from GM, or from the Fiero Store.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bill Osmer||10-31-2000||09:31 AM||||Based on your comment below, I looked for access panels on my 4-spd, but could find none. Are there only access plates on the 5-spd??<P>"SNIP"<BR>Well a few weeks later he could no longer shift into R or 5. I pulled the selector cover off the top of the trans and there was a large chunk of plastic jammed in the linkage. We removed that and all is fine for now. I think we will also remove the side cover and fish out any debris from there. ||||reg||5
Z||000004||2xsess||10-31-2000||12:26 PM||||Mine on my 4spd slipped of when I changed out a bad sensor, it looked like it floated away but there is a shelf like area right under there that it sat on. did you stick your finger in there and see if it was definetly gone?<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||10-31-2000||05:39 PM||||Bill,<BR> If it's the Muncie 4spd used with the v6, it will be different, and I've never had one apart. But if it's the Isuzu 4spd used with the 4cyl, it is almost identical to the Isuzu 5spd I worked on. The isuzus have a side cover that comes off easily without affecting any gears or bearings, and the gear selector housing on the top also unbolts easily (4 bolts).<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
